WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.904
Cost per 1,000 tasks, including the hardware.

2
00:00:04.184 --> 00:00:09.987
Quality differences are often small and uncertain; cost differences are often large and certain.

3
00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:20.464
Compute cost per 1,000 tasks for each system, because per-request prices are too small to reason about and per-month totals depend on volume.

4
00:00:20.744 --> 00:00:33.373
For an API model, cost per task is input tokens times the input price plus output tokens times the output price, using the token counts you recorded during the benchmark, not an estimate.

5
00:00:33.653 --> 00:00:46.347
Include everything a task actually consumes: system prompt and few-shot examples (often most of the input), retries after validation failures, and any reasoning tokens the model bills as output.

6
00:00:46.627 --> 00:00:50.360
Check the provider’s pricing page on the day you write the report.

7
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.587
As of this lesson’s review date, Anthropic lists Claude Haiku 5.

8
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.232
5 at $0.

9
00:00:56.512 --> 00:00:57.941
10 and $0.

10
00:00:58.221 --> 00:01:02.211
50 per million input and output tokens and Claude Sonnet 5.

11
00:01:02.491 --> 00:01:12.560
5 at $2 and $10, with a 50 percent discount for batch processing; those numbers change, which is why your report records the date and the source.

12
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:24.488
For a self-hosted model, the unit is GPU time, and the formula is the GPU’s hourly price divided by the tasks it completes per hour, times 1,000.

13
00:01:24.768 --> 00:01:26.603
Two inputs are easy to get wrong.

14
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:35.885
Throughput must be measured, at the batch size and concurrency you will actually run (lesson 11 explains why throughput depends so much on batching).

15
00:01:36.165 --> 00:01:39.835
Utilization is the share of paid hours that do useful work.

16
00:01:40.115 --> 00:01:53.469
A rented GPU bills whether or not requests arrive; Hugging Face Inference Endpoints, for example, bills per minute while an endpoint is running and lists one NVIDIA L4 on AWS at $0.

17
00:01:53.749 --> 00:01:55.861
80 per hour at the time of writing.

18
00:01:56.141 --> 00:02:04.376
If your traffic keeps the GPU busy 20 percent of the time, your effective cost per task is five times the busy-GPU figure.

19
00:02:04.656 --> 00:02:09.499
Scale-to-zero and batch scheduling change utilization, so say which you assume.

20
00:02:09.779 --> 00:02:18.717
Then name the costs that aren’t per task: engineering time to build and maintain serving, monitoring, on-call, and retraining when the task drifts.

21
00:02:18.997 --> 00:02:27.957
You don’t have to put a dollar figure on them, but a report that compares only GPU-hours to API tokens understates the cost of self-hosting.
